Description: antinomie: count queued file hashes as a run's pending work, also over a task's exit
 A run that hashes its writes (EXEC flag HASH_WRITES) hashes a file when
 its last writer lets it go, also at exit: the task closes its files in
 exit_files() and its task work, after ant_run_exit() has taken it out of
 the run. When the last task left, the run read as ended with nothing
 pending (TREE_EXITED and ASYNC_COMPLETE in RUN_STATUS) before those
 hashes were even queued, so a host that seals the run then had files
 the program left open with no hash ("still open at run end") and could
 not keep their bytes.
 .
 Each job queued for the hash worker (a hash, or a refusal the worker
 publishes) now counts in its run's pending asynchronous work from the
 moment it is queued until the worker has published or dropped its
 record. A task that leaves a run that hashes writes counts one pending
 operation from ant_run_exit(), under the same lock that may set
 TREE_EXITED, until its files are closed: a new hook in do_exit()
 (kernel/exit.c) after exit_task_work(), where the last fput of each of
 its files has run. A host that waits for the tree to end with nothing
 pending (every host since revision 17) therefore waits for those hashes,
 with no host change. io_uring workers have no root cookie and hash
 nothing, so they are not counted.
 .
 The wait is bounded by the hash queue's existing limits: at most
 ANTC_HASH_QUEUE (16) jobs and ANTC_HASH_PENDING_MAX (64 MiB) are read,
 files above ANTC_HASH_FILE_MAX (16 MiB) are not, and at most 64
 refusals wait to be published; a job past them is refused or dropped at
 once. An exit with many open files therefore adds at most that much
 reading to the run's end.
 .
 No contract change: RUN_STATUS keeps its layout, and its pending count
 was already "outstanding observed async work". The objects that change
 are kernel/exit.o, kernel/antinomie/run.o and kernel/antinomie/hash.o.
